A multifunction $F$ on $T\subset\Rb$ with (only) \emph{bounded closed/} values in $M$ and $iV(F,T)<+\infty$ may have no set-valued selectors $\Gamma$ satisfying  egin{equation} 
V(\Gamma,T_{[t_0,+\infty)})\leiV(F,T_{[t_0,+\infty)})\quad\mbox{or} \quad d_H(X_0,\Gamma(t_0))\le e(X_0,F(t_0)) \end{equation} with $t_0\in T$ and $X_0\in\mbox{c}(M)$. In order to see this, we set $T:=[0,1]$ and $X:=X_0\cup Y$, where $X_0:=\{u_1\}$ and $Y:=\{\alpha_nu_n:n\ge2\}$ with $\alpha_n:=1+(1/n)$, and note that $X$ and $Y$ are bounded and closed (but not compact) subsets of $M$, whereas $X_0\in\mbox{c}(M)$ and $X_0\subset X$. Define $F:T	o\{X,Y\}\subset\mathcal{P}(M)\setminus\{\es\}$ by $F(0):=X$ and $F(t):=Y$ if $0<t\le1$. (A similar example was given in \cite[Example~2]{JMAA} for $F$ with $
V(F,T)<+\infty$.) We have $iV(F,T)=e(X,Y)$, where  egin{align*} e(X,Y)&=\sup_{x\in X}\inf_{y\in Y}d(x,y)=e(X\setminus Y,Y)=e(X_0,Y)\ &=\inf_{n\ge2}(|u_1|+\alpha_n|u_n|)=1+\inf_{n\ge2}\Bigl(1+ rac1n\Bigr)=2. \end{align*} Since $Y\subset X$, $F$ is nonincreasing on $T$ (Section~ef{ss:mon}), and so, $\leV(F,T)=0$. (a) Suppose $t_0=0$, so that $T_{[t_0,+\infty)}=T$. We have $X_0\subset X=F(0)$, which implies $e(X_0,F(0))=0$. Let $\Gamma:T	o\mathcal{P}(M)\setminus\{\es\}$ be \emph{any/} set-valued selector of $F$ on $T$ such that $\Gamma(0)=X_0$. Since $\es
e\Gamma(1)\subset F(1)=Y$, $\alpha_nu_n\in\Gamma(1)$ for some $n\ge2$. It follows that  egin{align*} 
V(\Gamma,T)&\ge d_H(\Gamma(0),\Gamma(1))\ge e(\Gamma(1),\Gamma(0)) =e(\Gamma(1),X_0)\[1pt] &\ge d(\alpha_nu_n,u_1)=\alpha_n+1>2=iV(F,T). \end{align*} (b) Now, suppose $0<t_0\le1$, and $\Gamma:T	o\mathcal{P}(M)\setminus\{\es\}$ is a set-valued selector of $F$ on $T$. Since $\Gamma(t_0)\subset F(t_0)=Y$, we have $\alpha_nu_n\in\Gamma(t_0)$ for some $n\ge2$, and so,  egin{align*} d_H(X_0,\Gamma(t_0))&\ge e(\Gamma(t_0),X_0)\ge d(\alpha_nu_n,u_1) =\alpha_n+1\[3pt] &>2=e(X_0,Y)=e(X_0,F(t_0)). \end{align*} (c) The effect of nonexistence of set-valued selectors in (a) and (b) above is due to the fact that $\mbox{Pr}_YX_0=\es$ (cf.~\eq{e:Pr}): indeed, if $y\in Y$, then $y=\alpha_nu_n$ for some $n\ge2$, and so, for $x\in X_0=\{u_1\}$, we have  egin{equation*} d(x,y)=d(u_1,\alpha_nu_n)=1+\alpha_n>2=e(X_0,Y)=d(u_1,Y)=d(x,Y). \end{equation*}

This example is more subtle than Example~ef{ex1}: even if $F(t)$ is bounded and closed (but not compact) at a \emph{single/} point $t\in T$, inequalities \eq{e:zz} may not hold in Theorem~ef{t:BV} (this is inspired by \cite[Example~5.2]{Sovae}). Let $N\in\Nb$, $N\ge2$, be fixed and $\{\alpha_n\}_{n=1}^\infty\subset\Rb$ be a sequence such that  egin{equation} \mbox{$\{|\alpha_n|\}_{n=1}^\infty$ \,is \,strictly \,decreasing}\quad \mbox{and}\!\!\quad\inf_{n\ge N+1}|\alpha_n|>0 \end{equation} (e.g., $\alpha_n=\alpha(n+1)/n$ with $\alpha
e0$, $n\in\Nb$). We set $X:=\{\alpha_nu_n:1\le n\le N\}$ and $Y:=\{\alpha_nu_n:n\ge N+1\}$. Clearly, $X\in\mbox{c}(M)$, while $Y
otin\mbox{c}(M)$ is bounded (by the first condition in \eq{e:C1}) and closed (by the second condition in \eq{e:C1}) in $M$. Define $F$ on $T:=[0,1]$ by $F(t)\!:=X$ if $0\le t<1$, and $F(1)\!:=Y$. We have  egin{equation*} iV(F,T)=e(X,Y)=|\alpha_1|+\inf_{n\ge N+1}|\alpha_n| \end{equation*} and  egin{equation*} \leV(F,T)=e(Y,X)=|\alpha_{N+1}|+|\alpha_N|. \end{equation*} (a) Suppose $0\le t_0<1$ and $X_0:=\{\alpha_1u_1\}$, so that $T_{[t_0,+\infty)}=[t_0,1]$ and $X_0\subset X=F(t_0)$, which implies $e(X_0,F(t_0))=0$. Let $\Gamma:T	o\mathcal{P}(M)\setminus\{\es\}$ be a set-valued selector of $F$ on $T$ such that $\Gamma(t_0)=X_0$. Since $\Gamma(1)\subset F(1)=Y$, we find $\alpha_nu_n\in\Gamma(1)$ for some $n\ge N+1$, and so,  egin{align*} 
V(\Gamma,[t_0,1])&\ge d_H(\Gamma(t_0),\Gamma(1))\ge e (\Gamma(1),X_0) \ge d(\alpha_nu_n,\alpha_1u_1)=|\alpha_n|+|\alpha_1|\[2pt] &>|\alpha_1|+\inf_{i\ge N+1}|\alpha_i|=iV(F,T)=iV(F,[t_0,1]). \end{align*} (b) Suppose $t_0=1$ and $X_0:=\{\alpha_1u_1\}$. If $\Gamma:T	o\mathcal{P}(M)\setminus\{\es\}$ is a set-valued selector of $F$ on $T$, then $\Gamma(1)\subset F(1)=Y$ implies $\alpha_nu_n\in\Gamma(1)$ for some $n\ge N+1$, and so,  egin{align*} d_H(X_0,\Gamma(1))&\ge e(\Gamma(1),X_0)\ge d(\alpha_nu_n,\alpha_1u_1) =|\alpha_n|+|\alpha_1|\[2pt] &>|\alpha_1|+\inf_{i\ge N+1}|\alpha_i|=e(X,Y)\ge e(X_0,Y)=e(X_0,F(t_0)). \end{align*} (c) Clearly, $\mbox{Pr}_XX_0=X_0$. The non-existence of set-valued selectors in (a) and (b) is again due to the fact that $\mbox{Pr}_YX_0=\es$: in fact, if $y\in Y$, then $y=\alpha_nu_n$ for some $n\ge N+1$, and so, we have, for $x\in X_0=\{\alpha_1u_1\}$,  egin{equation*} d(x,y)\!=\!d(\alpha_1u_1,\alpha_nu_n)\!=\!|\alpha_1|+|\alpha_n| \!>\!\!\inf_{i\ge N+1}(|\alpha_1|+|\alpha_i|)\!=\!\inf_{y\in Y}d(x,y)\!=\!d(x,Y). \end{equation*} (d) We claim that Theorem~ef{t:BV}(b) holds with $t_0=1$ and $\Gamma(1)=X_0$ (except that $\Gamma(1)\in\mbox{c}(M)$) for every nonempty $X_0\subset Y=F(1)$. First, observe that $\mbox{Pr}_XY_0=\{\alpha_Nu_N\}$ for every $\es
e Y_0\subset Y$ (recall that $X\in\mbox{c}(M)$, and $\mbox{Pr}_XY_0$ is the set of those $x\in X$, for which $d(y_0,x)=d(y_0,X)$ for some $y_0\in Y_0$). To see this, we set $n_0:=\min\{n\ge N+1:\alpha_nu_n\in Y_0\}$ for $Y_0\subset Y$. If $y_0\in Y_0$, we have $y_0=\alpha_nu_n$ for some $n\ge n_0$,  egin{equation*} d(y_0,X)=\inf_{x\in X}d(y_0,x)=\min_{1\le i\le N}(|\alpha_n|+|\alpha_i|) =|\alpha_n|+|\alpha_N|,\[2pt] \end{equation*} and  egin{equation*} \mbox{$d(y_0,x)=|\alpha_n|+|\alpha_i|$ \,if \,$x=\alpha_iu_i\in X$ for some $1\le i\le N$.} \end{equation*} If $n=n_0$ and $i=N$, we find $y_0=\alpha_{n_0}u_{n_0}\in Y_0$, $x=\alpha_Nu_N\in X$, and  egin{equation*} d(y_0,x)=d(\alpha_{n_0}u_{n_0},\alpha_Nu_N)=|\alpha_{n_0}|+|\alpha_N|=d(y_0,X), \end{equation*} which implies $\alpha_Nu_N\in\mbox{Pr}_XY_0$. Now, if $i<N$, then $|\alpha_i|>|\alpha_N|$, and so, for every $y_0\in Y_0$, we get  egin{equation*} d(y_0,x)=|\alpha_n|+|\alpha_i|>|\alpha_n|+|\alpha_N|=d(y_0,X). \end{equation*} Thus, $\alpha_iu_i
otin\mbox{Pr}_XY_0$ for all $i=1,\dots,N-1$, and we are through. Taking the above into account, define a set-valued selector $\Gamma$ of $F$ on $T$ by $\Gamma(t):=\{\alpha_Nu_N\}$ if $0\le t<1$, and $\Gamma(1):=X_0$ with $X_0=Y_0\subset Y$. It remains to note that $n_0\ge N+1$ implies  egin{align*} \leV(\Gamma,T)&=e(Y_0,\{\alpha_Nu_N\})=\sup_{y_0\in Y_0}d(y_0,\alpha_Nu_N) =|\alpha_{n_0}|+|\alpha_N|\ &\le|\alpha_{N+1}|+|\alpha_N|=\leV(F,T). \end{align*}
